## Manual Verification Queries

Run these yourself in any Postgres client (or `psql`):

```sql
-- All maintenance tickets
SELECT ticket_id, tenant_id, unit_id, category, urgency, status, created_at
FROM maintenance_tickets
ORDER BY created_at;

-- All audit log entries
SELECT log_id, action, actor, details, before_state, after_state, timestamp
FROM audit_logs
ORDER BY timestamp;

-- Verify idempotency: count rows per idempotency_key (should always be 1)
SELECT idempotency_key, COUNT(*) AS row_count
FROM maintenance_tickets
GROUP BY idempotency_key
HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;  -- should return 0 rows

-- Verify property_id resolved correctly via join
SELECT t.ticket_id, t.unit_id, t.property_id, u.property_id AS unit_property_id
FROM maintenance_tickets t
JOIN units u ON u.unit_id = t.unit_id;  -- property_id columns should match
```

